  abstract = {Samples of water, sediments, vegetation and fish from humic influenced acidic lakes in southern Norway were analyzed for heavy metals including Cu, Pb, Cd, Zn, Cr and Mo. The values for water were (in .mu.g/l): Pb 1.0-2.2, Cu 3.1-50.0, Cd 0.3-1.0, Cr {$<$} 0.5-1.5. This agreed with the background levels in Norway, and probably represented no metabolic stress on freshwater organisms, since most metals could be chelated, or danger to drinking water. Vegetation accumulated considerable amounts of Pb and Cu, up to 800 .mu.g/g dry wt. The sediment accumulation represented normal backgrounds for unpolluted areas, or were slightly higher. These levels did not affect benthic animal distribution, since pH was always higher in sediments in acidic lakes than in the water, and the major fraction of the metal species could be chelated. Concentrations of heavy metals in fish were high in the acidic lakes, close to that recommended by WHO for fish for consumption. Fish (},
